{"results":[{"entries":[{"dbId":"9614396","stId":"R-HSA-9614396","id":"R-HSA-9614396","name":"<span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span>","type":"Protein","exactType":"ReferenceGeneProduct","species":["Homo sapiens"],"referenceName":"<span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span>","referenceIdentifier":"A8MYZ6","compartmentNames":["nucleoplasm"],"compartmentAccession":["0005654"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":true,"databaseName":"UniProt","referenceURL":"http://purl.uniprot.org/uniprot/A8MYZ6","disease":false},{"dbId":"9614995","stId":"R-HSA-9614995","id":"R-HSA-9614995","name":"p-T26,S184-<span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span>","type":"Protein","exactType":"ReferenceGeneProduct","species":["Homo sapiens"],"referenceName":"<span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span>","referenceIdentifier":"A8MYZ6","compartmentNames":["nucleoplasm"],"compartmentAccession":["0005654"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":true,"databaseName":"UniProt","referenceURL":"http://purl.uniprot.org/uniprot/A8MYZ6","disease":false}],"typeName":"Protein","entriesCount":2,"rowCount":2},{"entries":[{"dbId":"9615015","stId":"R-HSA-9615015","id":"R-HSA-9615015","name":"<span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span> binds PLXNA4 gene","type":"Reaction","exactType":"binding","species":["Homo sapiens"],"summation":"Based on studies in mice, <span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span> binds FOXO response elements in the PLXNA4 gene locus, encoding the semaphorin SEMA3A co-receptor PLXNA4. FOXO response elements are found in the promoter and the first intron of the mouse Plxna4 gene (Paap et al. 2016). The first intron of the human PLXNA4 gene contains several predicted FOXO response elements.","compartmentNames":["nucleoplasm"],"compartmentAccession":["0005654"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false},{"dbId":"9622751","stId":"R-NUL-9622751","id":"R-NUL-9622751","name":"<span class=\"highlighting\" >Foxo6</span> binds CAT gene promoter","type":"Reaction","exactType":"binding","species":["Mus musculus","Homo sapiens"],"summation":"Recombinant mouse <span class=\"highlighting\" >Foxo6</span> binds the promoter of the human CAT gene, encoding catalase (Kim et al. 2014).","compartmentNames":["nucleoplasm"],"compartmentAccession":["0005654"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false},{"dbId":"9622814","stId":"R-NUL-9622814","id":"R-NUL-9622814","name":"CAT gene expression is stimulated by <span class=\"highlighting\" >Foxo6</span>","type":"Reaction","exactType":"omitted","species":["Mus musculus","Homo sapiens"],"summation":"Transcription of the human CAT gene, encoding catalase, is directly stimulated by recombinant mouse <span class=\"highlighting\" >Foxo6</span> (Kim et al. 2014).","compartmentNames":["nucleoplasm","cytosol"],"compartmentAccession":["0005654","0005829"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false},{"dbId":"9615023","stId":"R-HSA-9615023","id":"R-HSA-9615023","name":"PLXNA4 gene expression is stimulated by <span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span>","type":"Reaction","exactType":"omitted","species":["Homo sapiens"],"summation":"Based on studies in mice, <span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span> directly stimulates transcription of the semaphorin SEMA3A co-receptor PLXNA4 (Plexin A4). Defects in migration of the radial glia during cortical development observed in <span class=\"highlighting\" >Foxo6</span> knockout mice can be restored by ectopic overexpression of Plxna4 (Paap et al. 2016). ","compartmentNames":["nucleoplasm"],"compartmentAccession":["0005654"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false},{"dbId":"9622738","stId":"R-HSA-9622738","id":"R-HSA-9622738","name":"CAT gene expression is stimulated by FOXO3,<span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span>,(FOXO1)","type":"Reaction","exactType":"omitted","species":["Homo sapiens"],"summation":"Transcription of the CAT gene is directly stimulated by FOXO3 (Rangarajan et al. 2015, Song et al. 2016) and <span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span> (Kim et al. 2014), and is positively regulated by FOXO1 (Awad et al. 2014, Liao et al. 2016, Guo et al. 2016), although direct regulation by FOXO1 has not been demonstrated. CAT encodes the enzyme catalase, which converts hydrogen peroxide to water and oxygen, thus protecting cells from the oxidative stress.","compartmentNames":["nucleoplasm","cytosol"],"compartmentAccession":["0005654","0005829"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false},{"dbId":"9622737","stId":"R-HSA-9622737","id":"R-HSA-9622737","name":"FOXO3,<span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span>,(FOXO1) binds CAT gene promoter","type":"Reaction","exactType":"binding","species":["Homo sapiens"],"summation":"Based on studies with mouse FOXO proteins, FOXO3 (Rangarajan et al. 2015, Song et al. 2016) and <span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span> (Kim et al. 2014) bind forkhead box elements in the promoter of the CAT gene, encoding the enzyme catalase. Catalase converts hydrogen peroxide to water and oxygen, thus protecting cells from the oxidative stress. FOXO1 positively regulates CAT gene transcription (Awad et al. 2014, Liao et al. 2016, Guo et al. 2016) and is probably able to bind to forkhead box elements in the CAT gene promoter.<br>FOXO3-mediated upregulation of the CAT gene transcription is positively regulated by SIRT3 histone deacetylase, which deacetylates FOXO3 under conditions of oxidative stress and increases nuclear localization of FOXO3 (Sundaresan et al. 2009, Rangarajan et al. 2015, Yang et al. 2016). ","compartmentNames":["nucleoplasm"],"compartmentAccession":["0005654"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false},{"dbId":"9623192","stId":"R-HSA-9623192","id":"R-HSA-9623192","name":"G6PC gene expression is stimulated by FOXO1,FOXO3,FOXO4,<span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span>","type":"Reaction","exactType":"omitted","species":["Homo sapiens"],"summation":"FOXO1 (Onuma et al. 2006), which can function in collaboration with PPARGC1A (PGC-1alpha) (Puigserver et al. 2003), FOXO3 (Onuma et al. 2006), FOXO4 (Yang et al. 2002) and <span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span> (Kim et al. 2011) directly stimulate transcription of the G6PC gene, encoding Glucose-6-phosphatase. G6PC generates glucose and enables maintenance of steady glucose blood levels during fasting. <span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span> mRNA levels increase in liver cells during fasting (Kim et al. 2011). Upregulation of G6PC expression by FOXO1 and <span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span> is inhibited by insulin. FOXO-mediated induction of G6PC is involved in excessive endogenous glucose production and fasting hyperglycemia in diabetes (Kim et al. 2011).","compartmentNames":["nucleoplasm","cytosol"],"compartmentAccession":["0005654","0005829"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false},{"dbId":"9623168","stId":"R-HSA-9623168","id":"R-HSA-9623168","name":"FOXO1,FOXO1:PPARGC1A,FOXO3,FOXO4,<span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span> bind G6PC gene promoter","type":"Reaction","exactType":"binding","species":["Homo sapiens"],"summation":"FOXO1 (Ayala et al. 1999, Onuma et al. 2006, Puigserver et al. 2003), FOXO3 (Onuma et al. 2006), FOXO4 (Yang et al. 2002) and <span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span> (Kim et al. 2011) bind insulin response elements in the promoter of the G6PC gene, encoding Glucose-6-phosphatase. Formation of the complex between FOXO1 and PPARGC1A (PGC-1alpha) may be required for FOXO1 to bind the G6PC gene promoter (Puigserver et al. 2003). Binding of FOXO transcription factors to the G6PC gene promoter is negatively regulated by insulin.","compartmentNames":["nucleoplasm"],"compartmentAccession":["0005654"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false},{"dbId":"199299","stId":"R-HSA-199299","id":"R-HSA-199299","name":"AKT phosphorylates FOXO transcription factors","type":"Reaction","exactType":"transition","species":["Homo sapiens"],"summation":"AKT-mediated phosphorylation of Forkhead box (FOX) transcription factors of the FOXO family, FOXO1 (FKHR), FOXO3 (FoxO3a, also known as FKHRL1) and FOXO4 (AFX) contributes to PI3K/AKT signaling-stimulated cell survival and growth. Activated AKT1 phosphorylates FOXO1 on threonine residue T24 and serine residues S256 and S319 (Rena et al. 1999), FOXO3 on threonine residue T32 and serine residues S253 and S315 (Brunet et al. 1999), and FOXO4 on threonine residue T32 and serine residues S197 and S262 (Kops et al. 1999).<br>Based on studies with recombinant mouse <span class=\"highlighting\" >Foxo6</span> expressed in the human embryonic kidney cell line HEK293, <span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span> has two conserved AKT phosphorylation sites: T26 and S184. Mouse <span class=\"highlighting\" >Foxo6</span> has a third predicted Akt phosphorylation site at the C-terminus, T338, which is not present in other Foxo family members and is not conserved in human <span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span>. T26 and S184 are phosphorylated in response to growth factors known to activate PI3K/AKT signaling, but AKT has not been explicitly identified as the responsible kinase. In contrast to other FOXO family members, <span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span> remains predominantly nuclear irrespective of growth factor-induced signaling, and only a small portion of phosphorylated <span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span> may shuttle to the cytosol. Phosphorylation of <span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span> on putative AKT sites, however, may inhibit binding of <span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span> to target DNA sites (Jacobs et al. 2003, van der Heide et al. 2005).<br>Protein phosphatase DUSP6 (MKP3) may act to dephosphorylate FOXO1 after AKT-mediated phosphorylation (Rodrigues et al. 2017). ","compartmentNames":["nucleoplasm"],"compartmentAccession":["0005654"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false},{"dbId":"9614414","stId":"R-HSA-9614414","id":"R-HSA-9614414","name":"AKT-phosphorylated FOXO1,FOXO3,FOXO4 translocate to the cytosol","type":"Reaction","exactType":"omitted","species":["Homo sapiens"],"summation":"AKT-mediated phosphorylation results in exclusion of FOXO1 (Rena et al. 2001), FOXO3 (Brunet et al. 1999) and FOXO4 (Obslova et al. 2005) from the nucleus. In the absence of PI3K/AKT signaling, FOXO1 (Rena et al. 2001), FOXO3 (Brunet et al. 1999) and FOXO4 (Matsuzaki et al. 2005) are mainly nuclear, but after activation of PI3K/AKT signaling, they become cytoplasmic. One of the conserved AKT phosphorylation sites is within the nuclear localization signal (NLS) of FOXO1, FOXO3 and FOXO4.<br>In contrast to other FOXO family members, <span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span> remains predominantly nuclear irrespective of growth factor-induced signaling, and only a small portion of <span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span> phosphorylated on putative AKT sites may shuttle to the nucleus (Jacobs et al. 2003, van der Heide et al. 2005). ","compartmentNames":["nucleoplasm","cytosol"],"compartmentAccession":["0005654","0005829"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false}],"typeName":"Reaction","entriesCount":11,"rowCount":10},{"entries":[{"dbId":"9622805","stId":"R-HSA-9622805","id":"R-HSA-9622805","name":"FOXO3,<span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span>,(FOXO1)","type":"Set","exactType":"CandidateSet","species":["Homo sapiens"],"compartmentNames":["nucleoplasm"],"compartmentAccession":["0005654"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false},{"dbId":"199272","stId":"R-HSA-199272","id":"R-HSA-199272","name":"FOXO1,FOXO3,FOXO4,(<span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span>)","type":"Set","exactType":"CandidateSet","species":["Homo sapiens"],"compartmentNames":["nucleoplasm"],"compartmentAccession":["0005654"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false},{"dbId":"9623256","stId":"R-HSA-9623256","id":"R-HSA-9623256","name":"FOXO1,FOXO1:PPARGC1A,FOXO3,FOXO4,<span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span>","type":"Set","exactType":"DefinedSet","species":["Homo sapiens"],"compartmentNames":["nucleoplasm"],"compartmentAccession":["0005654"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false},{"dbId":"9614997","stId":"R-HSA-9614997","id":"R-HSA-9614997","name":"p-T24,S256,S319-FOXO1,p-T32,S253,S315-FOXO3,p-T32,S197,S262-FOXO4,(p-T26,S184-<span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span>)","type":"Set","exactType":"CandidateSet","species":["Homo sapiens"],"compartmentNames":["nucleoplasm"],"compartmentAccession":["0005654"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false}],"typeName":"Set","entriesCount":4,"rowCount":4},{"entries":[{"dbId":"9622794","stId":"R-NUL-9622794","id":"R-NUL-9622794","name":"<span class=\"highlighting\" >Foxo6</span>:CAT gene","type":"Complex","exactType":"Complex","species":["Mus musculus","Homo sapiens"],"compartmentNames":["nucleoplasm"],"compartmentAccession":["0005654"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false},{"dbId":"9615222","stId":"R-HSA-9615222","id":"R-HSA-9615222","name":"<span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span>:PLXNA4 gene","type":"Complex","exactType":"Complex","species":["Homo sapiens"],"compartmentNames":["nucleoplasm"],"compartmentAccession":["0005654"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false},{"dbId":"9622806","stId":"R-HSA-9622806","id":"R-HSA-9622806","name":"FOXO3,<span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span>,(FOXO1):CAT gene","type":"Complex","exactType":"Complex","species":["Homo sapiens"],"compartmentNames":["nucleoplasm"],"compartmentAccession":["0005654"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false},{"dbId":"9623250","stId":"R-HSA-9623250","id":"R-HSA-9623250","name":"FOXO1,FOXO1:PPARGC1A,FOXO3,FOXO4,<span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span>:G6PC gene","type":"Complex","exactType":"Complex","species":["Homo sapiens"],"compartmentNames":["nucleoplasm"],"compartmentAccession":["0005654"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false}],"typeName":"Complex","entriesCount":4,"rowCount":4},{"entries":[{"dbId":"9614399","stId":"R-HSA-9614399","id":"R-HSA-9614399","name":"Regulation of localization of FOXO transcription factors","type":"Pathway","exactType":"Pathway","species":["Homo sapiens"],"summation":"Localization of FOXO transcription factors FOXO1, FOXO3 and FOXO4 is regulated by AKT-mediated phosphorylation. In the absence of PI3K/AKT signaling, FOXO1, FOXO3 and FOXO4 localize to the nucleus. AKT-mediated phosphorylation induces a conformational change that exposes a nuclear export signal (NES) and promotes translocation of FOXO1, FOXO3 and FOXO4 to the cytosol (Rena et al. 1999, Brunet et al. 1999, Kops et al. 1999). AKT-phosphorylated FOXO1, FOXO3 and FOXO4 bind to 14-3-3 proteins, which contributes to their retention in the cytosol (Rena et al. 2001, Brunet et al. 1999, Arimoto Ishida et al. 2004, Obsilova et al. 2005, Boura et al. 2007, Silhan et al. 2009). <span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span> lacks the NES sequence and is exclusively nuclear, but phosphorylation in response to PI3K/AKT signaling affects the transcriptional activity of <span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span> (Jacobs et al. 2003, van der Heide et al. 2005).","isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"hasEHLD":false,"disease":false},{"dbId":"9615017","stId":"R-HSA-9615017","id":"R-HSA-9615017","name":"FOXO-mediated transcription of oxidative stress, metabolic and neuronal genes","type":"Pathway","exactType":"Pathway","species":["Homo sapiens"],"summation":"<span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span>, the least studied member of the FOXO family, directly stimulates transcription of PLXNA4 gene, encoding a co-factor for the semaphorin SEMA3A receptor. <span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span>-mediated regulation of PLXNA4 expression plays an important role in radial glia migration during cortical development (Paap et al. 2016).<br>FOXO-mediated up-regulation of genes involved in reduction of the oxidative stress burden is not specific to neurons, but plays an important role in neuronal survival and neurodegenerative diseases. FOXO3 and FOXO4, and possibly FOXO1, directly stimulate transcription of the SOD2 gene, encoding mitochondrial manganese-dependent superoxide dismutase, which converts superoxide to the less harmful hydrogen peroxide and oxygen (Kops et al. 2002, Hori et al. 2013, Araujo et al. 2011, Guan et al. 2016). FOXO4 stimulates SOD2 gene transcription in collaboration with ATXN3, a protein involved in spinocerebellar ataxia type 3 (SCA3) (Araujo et al. 2011). FOXO3 and <span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span>, and possibly FOXO1, directly stimulate transcription of the CAT gene, encoding catalase, an enzyme that converts hydrogen peroxide to water and oxygen, thus protecting cells from the oxidative stress (Awad et al. 2014, Kim et al. 2014, Rangarajan et al. 2015, Song et al. 2016, Liao et al. 2016, Guo et al. 2016).<br>FOXO transcription factors regulate transcription of several genes whose protein products are secreted from hypothalamic neurons to control appetite and food intake: NPY gene, AGRP gene and POMC gene. At low insulin levels, characteristic of starvation, FOXO transcription factors bind to insulin responsive elements (IRES) in the regulatory regions of NPY, AGRP and POMC gene. FOXO1 directly stimulates transcription of the NPY gene, encoding neuropeptide-Y (Kim et al. 2006, Hong et al. 2012), and the AGRP gene, encoding Agouti-related protein (Kitamura et al. 2006, Kim et al. 2006), which both stimulate food intake. At the same time, FOXO1 directly represses transcription of the POMC gene, encoding melanocyte stimulating hormone alpha , which suppresses food intake (Kitamura et al. 2006, Kim et al. 2006). When, upon food intake, blood insulin levels rise, insulin-mediated activation of PI3K/AKT signaling inhibits FOXO transcriptional activity.<br>In liver cells, FOXO transcription factors regulate transcription of genes involved in gluconeogenesis: G6PC gene, encoding glucose-6-phosphatase and PCK1 gene, encoding phosphoenolpyruvate carboxykinase. Actions of G6PC and PCK1 enable steady glucose blood levels during fasting. FOXO1, FOXO3 and FOXO4 directly stimulate PCK1 gene transcription (Hall et al. 2000, Yang et al. 2002, Puigserver et al. 2003), while all four FOXOs, FOXO1, FOXO3, FOXO4 and <span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span> directly stimulate G6PC gene transcription (Yang et al. 2002, Puigserver et al. 2003, Onuma et al. 2006, Kim et al. 2011). FOXO-mediated induction of G6PC and PCK1 genes is negatively regulated by insulin-induced PI3K/AKT signaling.<br>FOXO1, FOXO3 and FOXO4 directly stimulate transcription of the IGFBP1 gene, encoding insulin growth factor binding protein 2 (Tang et al. 1999, Kops et al. 1999, Hall et al. 2000, Yang et al. 2002), which increases sensitivity of cells to insulin.<br>FOXO1 and FOXO3 directly stimulate transcription of the ABCA6 (ATP-binding cassette sub-family A member 6) gene, encoding a putative transporter protein that is thought to be involved in lipid homeostasis (Gai et al. 2013). The GCK (glucokinase) gene is another gene involved in lipid homeostasis that is regulated by FOXOs. FOXO1, acting with the SIN3A:HDAC complex, directly represses the GCK gene transcription, thus repressing lipogenesis in the absence of insulin (Langlet et al. 2017). The SREBF1 (SREBP1) gene, which encodes a transcriptional activator required for lipid homeostasis, is directly transcriptionally repressed by FOXO1 (Deng et al. 2012). Transcription of the RETN gene, encoding resistin, an adipocyte specific hormone that suppresses insulin-mediated uptake of glucose by adipose cells, is directly stimulated by FOXO1 (Liu et al. 2014).<br>Transcription of two genes encoding E3 ubiquitin ligases FBXO32 (Atrogin-1) and TRIM63 (MURF1), involved in degradation of muscle proteins and muscle wasting during starvation, is positively regulated by FOXO transcription factors (Sandri et al. 2004, Waddell et al. 2008, Raffaello et al. 2010, Senf et al. 2011, Bollinger et al. 2014, Wang et al. 2017).","isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"hasEHLD":false,"disease":false},{"dbId":"9614085","stId":"R-HSA-9614085","id":"R-HSA-9614085","name":"FOXO-mediated transcription","type":"Pathway","exactType":"Pathway","species":["Homo sapiens"],"summation":"The family of FOXO transcription factors includes FOXO1, FOXO3, FOXO4 and <span class=\"highlighting\" >FOXO6</span>. FOXO transcription factors integrate pathways that regulate cell survival, growth, differentiation and metabolism in response to environmental changes, such as growth factor deprivation, starvation and oxidative stress (reviewed by Accili and Arden 2004, Calnan and Brunet 2008, Eijkelenboom and Burgering 2013).","isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"hasEHLD":false,"disease":false}],"typeName":"Pathway","entriesCount":3,"rowCount":3},{"entries":[{"dbId":"014154","stId":"R-ICO-014154","id":"R-ICO-014154","name":"FOXO","type":"Icon","exactType":"Icon","species":["Homo sapiens"],"summation":"Generic representation of Forkhead box protein O1, O3, O4 and/or O6","hasReferenceEntity":false,"hasEHLD":false,"iconName":"FOXO","iconCategories":["protein"],"iconCuratorName":"Marija Orlic-Milacic","iconCuratorOrcidId":"0000-0002-3218-5631","iconDesignerName":"Cristoffer Sevilla","iconDesignerUrl":"https://graphsolutions.co.uk","iconReferences":["Q12778","UNIPROT:Q12778","O43524","UNIPROT:O43524","P98177","UNIPROT:P98177","A8MYZ6","UNIPROT:A8MYZ6"],"iconPhysicalEntities":[{"stId":"R-HSA-9617761","type":"ReferenceGeneProduct","name":"Ac-K-FOXO4","compartments":"nucleoplasm","displayName":"Ac-K-FOXO4 [nucleoplasm]"},{"stId":"R-HSA-9620514","type":"ReferenceGeneProduct","name":"Ac-K242,K259,K271,K290,K569-FOXO3","compartments":"nucleoplasm","displayName":"Ac-K242,K259,K271,K290,K569-FOXO3 [nucleoplasm]"},{"stId":"R-HSA-9626942","type":"ReferenceGeneProduct","name":"Ac-K245,K248,K265-FOXO1","compartments":"nucleoplasm","displayName":"Ac-K245,K248,K265-FOXO1 [nucleoplasm]"},{"stId":"R-HSA-9841849","type":"ReferenceGeneProduct","name":"Ac-K271,K290-FOXO3(?-673)","compartments":"mitochondrial matrix","displayName":"Ac-K271,K290-FOXO3(?-673) [mitochondrial matrix]"},{"stId":"R-HSA-9617679","type":"ReferenceGeneProduct","name":"CREBBP-S-S-FOXO4","compartments":"nucleoplasm","displayName":"CREBBP-S-S-FOXO4 [nucleoplasm]"},{"stId":"R-HSA-9617681","type":"ReferenceGeneProduct","name":"EP300-S-S-FOXO4","compartments":"nucleoplasm","displayName":"EP300-S-S-FOXO4 [nucleoplasm]"},{"stId":"R-HSA-199300","type":"ReferenceGeneProduct","name":"FOXO1","compartments":"nucleoplasm","displayName":"FOXO1 [nucleoplasm]"},{"stId":"uniprot:Q12778","type":"ReferenceGeneProduct","name":"FOXO1","compartments":"null","displayName":"FOXO1 [null]"},{"stId":"R-HSA-5687032","type":"ReferenceGeneProduct","name":"FOXO3","compartments":"cytosol","displayName":"FOXO3 [cytosol]"},{"stId":"R-HSA-199305","type":"ReferenceGeneProduct","name":"FOXO3","compartments":"nucleoplasm","displayName":"FOXO3 [nucleoplasm]"},{"stId":"uniprot:O43524","type":"ReferenceGeneProduct","name":"FOXO3","compartments":"null","displayName":"FOXO3 [null]"},{"stId":"R-HSA-9841824","type":"ReferenceGeneProduct","name":"FOXO3(?-673)","compartments":"mitochondrial matrix","displayName":"FOXO3(?-673) [mitochondrial matrix]"},{"stId":"R-HSA-5690183","type":"ReferenceGeneProduct","name":"FOXO4","compartments":"nucleoplasm","displayName":"FOXO4 [nucleoplasm]"},{"stId":"uniprot:P98177","type":"ReferenceGeneProduct","name":"FOXO4","compartments":"null","displayName":"FOXO4 [null]"},{"stId":"R-HSA-9614396","type":"ReferenceGeneProduct","name":"FOXO6","compartments":"nucleoplasm","displayName":"FOXO6 [nucleoplasm]"},{"stId":"uniprot:A8MYZ6","type":"ReferenceGeneProduct","name":"FOXO6","compartments":"null","displayName":"FOXO6 [null]"},{"stId":"R-HSA-5687014","type":"ReferenceGeneProduct","name":"p-S215 FOXO3","compartments":"cytosol","displayName":"p-S215 FOXO3 [cytosol]"},{"stId":"R-HSA-5687010","type":"ReferenceGeneProduct","name":"p-S215 FOXO3","compartments":"nucleoplasm","displayName":"p-S215 FOXO3 [nucleoplasm]"},{"stId":"R-HSA-5692651","type":"ReferenceGeneProduct","name":"p-S218-FOXO1","compartments":"nucleoplasm","displayName":"p-S218-FOXO1 [nucleoplasm]"},{"stId":"R-HSA-8870560","type":"ReferenceGeneProduct","name":"p-S43,S173,S294,S325-FOXO3","compartments":"cytosol","displayName":"p-S43,S173,S294,S325-FOXO3 [cytosol]"},{"stId":"R-HSA-8870627","type":"ReferenceGeneProduct","name":"p-S43,S173,S294,S325-FOXO3","compartments":"nucleoplasm","displayName":"p-S43,S173,S294,S325-FOXO3 [nucleoplasm]"},{"stId":"R-HSA-211158","type":"ReferenceGeneProduct","name":"p-T24,S256,S319-FOXO1","compartments":"cytosol","displayName":"p-T24,S256,S319-FOXO1 [cytosol]"},{"stId":"R-HSA-199306","type":"ReferenceGeneProduct","name":"p-T24,S256,S319-FOXO1","compartments":"nucleoplasm","displayName":"p-T24,S256,S319-FOXO1 [nucleoplasm]"},{"stId":"R-HSA-9614995","type":"ReferenceGeneProduct","name":"p-T26,S184-FOXO6","compartments":"nucleoplasm","displayName":"p-T26,S184-FOXO6 [nucleoplasm]"},{"stId":"R-HSA-9614415","type":"ReferenceGeneProduct","name":"p-T32,S197,S262-FOXO4","compartments":"cytosol","displayName":"p-T32,S197,S262-FOXO4 [cytosol]"},{"stId":"R-HSA-199314","type":"ReferenceGeneProduct","name":"p-T32,S197,S262-FOXO4","compartments":"nucleoplasm","displayName":"p-T32,S197,S262-FOXO4 [nucleoplasm]"},{"stId":"R-HSA-9614413","type":"ReferenceGeneProduct","name":"p-T32,S253,S315-FOXO3","compartments":"cytosol","displayName":"p-T32,S253,S315-FOXO3 [cytosol]"},{"stId":"R-HSA-199289","type":"ReferenceGeneProduct","name":"p-T32,S253,S315-FOXO3","compartments":"nucleoplasm","displayName":"p-T32,S253,S315-FOXO3 [nucleoplasm]"},{"stId":"R-HSA-6782501","type":"ReferenceGeneProduct","name":"PolyUb-FOXO4","compartments":"nucleoplasm","displayName":"PolyUb-FOXO4 [nucleoplasm]"},{"stId":"R-HSA-9617655","type":"ReferenceGeneProduct","name":"SOH-C481-FOXO4","compartments":"nucleoplasm","displayName":"SOH-C481-FOXO4 [nucleoplasm]"}],"iconEhlds":["R-HSA-2219528"],"icon":true}],"typeName":"Icon","entriesCount":1,"rowCount":1}],"rowCount":24,"numberOfGroups":6,"numberOfMatches":25}